      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my realtor just mentioned something about needing an escrow account for this house i'm looking at in toronto. i've never bought a place before so i'm kinda lost. can u explain what escrow actually is and why it's even used in the closing process?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow, in the context of real estate, is a trust account that holds funds until certain conditions are met. It's a way to ensure that all parties involved in a transaction, like the buyer and seller, fulfill their obligations. Think of it like a temporary holding cell for your money, where it's safely kept until the deal is closed and all the necessary paperwork is signed.\n\nIn the case of a home purchase, the escrow account typically holds funds for property taxes and insurance, which are usually paid annually. The idea is that the buyer and seller will split the costs evenly, and the escrow account will make sure that the funds are transferred when the time comes. It's a bit like a temporary stash of gold coins, where the buyer and seller agree to keep their end of the bargain until the transaction is complete.\n\nNow, in Toronto, the escrow account is usually managed by a third-party company, which is responsible for collecting and disbursing the funds. It's a bit like a team of skilled goblin accountants, working behind the scenes to ensure that everything runs smoothly.\n\nWhen you're buying a house, you'll typically need to fund the escrow account with a portion of the purchase price. This is usually",
